-- W9 (sub portal v3, round 1): the drafted agreement text.
--
-- NOT APPLIED. The orchestrator applies it after review.
--
-- Penney has no lawyer engaged (ADDENDUM 2), so Claude drafted the master
-- subcontract agreement, the work order and sub change order terms, the
-- acceptance statements and the e-sign consent: plain English, standard
-- residential GC practice, Massachusetts-aware (c.149 §148B, c.149 §29C,
-- c.152 §18, c.254 §32, c.110G, 454 CMR 22.00). Every business number or
-- choice Ryan must make is a "[FILL IN: ...]" blank, and every section opens
-- with a "[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review ...]" line. The DB check
-- approved_needs_review refuses approval while any "[FILL IN" remains, so the
-- text can only go live after Ryan reads every section, fills every blank and
-- taps Approve on /subcontractors/agreements (Ryan-only gate in the app).
--
-- Safe to re-run: each UPDATE only touches the untouched version-1 draft
-- (empty sections and the seeded placeholder text), so it never overwrites
-- anything Ryan has already edited in the app, and never an approved row.
-- The same text is in DRAFT-subcontract-agreement.md for Ryan to read.

update public.sub_agreement_templates
   set title = $penney_w9$Master subcontract agreement$penney_w9$,
       sections = $penney_w9$[{"title":"Who this agreement is between","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nThis Master Subcontract Agreement is between Penney Construction, Inc., 5 Barrett Road, Peabody, MA 01960, Massachusetts Home Improvement Contractor #198443 (\"Penney\"), and the company named on page one (\"Subcontractor\").\n\"Owner\" means the homeowner or client Penney is working for, and any condominium association with rules for the property. \"Work Order\" means a job-specific order Penney issues under this Agreement. \"Sub Change Order\" means a written change to a Work Order. \"Work\" means everything a Work Order asks Subcontractor to do."},{"title":"How work orders work","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nThis Agreement sets the terms for every job. Each job gets its own Work Order with the scope, price, dates and any Owner requirements for that job. Subcontractor does not have to accept a Work Order, and Penney does not have to issue one. Once both parties sign a Work Order, it is binding together with this Agreement.\nIf a Work Order and this Agreement conflict, the Work Order's job-specific scope, price, dates and Owner requirements control for that job, except that an Owner requirement never makes Subcontractor indemnify or answer for anyone beyond the Indemnity section, and never asks it to waive lien rights beyond the Lien waivers section. Everything else in this Agreement still applies.\nThis Agreement covers Work Orders issued during the calendar year shown on page one, and keeps applying to those Work Orders until their work is finished, warranted and paid."},{"title":"Independent contractor","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor is an independent business. It is free from Penney's control and direction in how it performs the Work, it is customarily engaged in its own established trade, and it is free to work for other customers. Subcontractor decides how the Work is done, supplies its own tools and equipment, and hires, pays, supervises and directs its own workers. Penney decides what the finished result must be, the schedule, and the site rules in this Agreement.\nSubcontractor is responsible for its own taxes, insurance, licenses and workers. Nothing in this Agreement makes Subcontractor, or anyone working for it, an employee, partner or agent of Penney, and Subcontractor will not present itself as one. (M.G.L. c.149 §148B.)"},{"title":"Licenses, permits and inspections","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor holds every Massachusetts license its trade requires, including any construction supervisor license its work needs, and keeps it current. Where the law requires the licensed trade to pull its own permit (for example electrical, plumbing and gas work), Subcontractor pulls that permit in its own name, pays the fee unless the Work Order says Penney pays it, books and attends its inspections, and closes the permit out.\nSubcontractor gives Penney its license numbers before starting and tells Penney right away if a license is suspended or lapses. Work that fails inspection is corrected at Subcontractor's cost."},{"title":"Lead-safe work and hazardous materials","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nIf the home was built before 1978, Subcontractor treats painted surfaces as containing lead unless a test shows otherwise. Any Work that disturbs paint, including demolition, follows the U.S. EPA Renovation, Repair and Painting Rule (40 CFR Part 745) and the Massachusetts lead-safe renovation rules (454 CMR 22.00). Subcontractor holds the required certifications and Massachusetts license, has a certified renovator directing the work, sets up containment, cleans up and checks the cleanup, and keeps the required records. It gives Penney copies of its certifications before starting, and its records when asked.\nIf Subcontractor finds anything that may be asbestos, mold or another hazardous material, it stops that part of the Work, does not disturb it, and tells Penney the same day."},{"title":"Insurance","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nBefore starting any Work, and for as long as it performs Work or warranty work, Subcontractor carries at its own cost:\n- commercial general liability insurance of at least [FILL IN: general liability limit each occurrence, e.g. $1,000,000] each occurrence and [FILL IN: general liability aggregate, e.g. $2,000,000] aggregate, including completed operations;\n- commercial auto liability insurance of at least [FILL IN: auto liability limit, e.g. $1,000,000 combined single limit] for owned, hired and non-owned vehicles;\n- workers' compensation insurance as Massachusetts law requires, with employer's liability of at least [FILL IN: employer's liability limits, e.g. $500,000 each accident / $500,000 disease each employee / $500,000 disease policy limit];\n- [FILL IN: umbrella or excess liability limit, or write \"No umbrella or excess policy is required.\"]\nThe general liability policy names Penney Construction, Inc., and any Owner or association a Work Order lists, as additional insureds for ongoing and completed operations, is primary and non-contributory to any insurance they carry, and waives subrogation against them.\nSubcontractor gives Penney a certificate of insurance showing all of this before starting, and a new one whenever a policy renews or changes, and tells Penney at once if a policy is cancelled or not renewed. While any required coverage is missing or expired, Subcontractor may not work on site and Penney may hold payment. Any lower-tier subcontractor Penney approves carries the same coverage."},{"title":"Workers' compensation","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nUnder M.G.L. c.152 §18, Penney can be held responsible for injuries to the workers of an uninsured subcontractor, so this section is strict.\nBefore starting, Subcontractor gives Penney either (a) a certificate showing workers' compensation coverage for everyone who will work on Penney jobs, or (b) if Subcontractor is a sole proprietor with no employees, the Massachusetts workers' compensation affidavit for sole proprietors saying so. A sole proprietor who gives the affidavit will not bring any helper, employee or lower-tier subcontractor onto a Penney job without first giving Penney a workers' compensation certificate that covers them.\nIf coverage lapses, Subcontractor stops work on Penney jobs until it is restored, and repays Penney any cost Penney bears because Subcontractor lacked coverage."},{"title":"Tax forms and paying workers","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nBefore Penney makes its first payment, Subcontractor gives Penney a signed IRS Form W-9 with its legal name and taxpayer ID number. Penney makes no payment until it has one, and withholds tax where the law requires. Penney reports payments to the IRS and Massachusetts as the law requires.\nSubcontractor pays its own income, payroll and other taxes, and pays its own workers in full and on time as the law requires, including the Massachusetts wage and hour laws (M.G.L. c.149 and c.151)."},{"title":"Quality of work","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor performs the Work in a good and workmanlike manner, following the drawings and specifications Penney provides, the building code, manufacturers' installation instructions, and accepted Massachusetts practice for its trade.\nBefore starting, Subcontractor checks the existing conditions and the drawings for its scope and tells Penney in writing about any conflict or problem before going ahead. Subcontractor promptly corrects, at its own cost, work that does not meet this standard, including any damage the correction causes to other work."},{"title":"Schedule","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor confirms the dates Penney offers in the sub portal, shows up on the dates it confirms with enough workers, materials and equipment to keep the Work moving, and coordinates with Penney's crew and the other trades.\nIf Subcontractor cannot make a confirmed date, or anything will delay the Work, it tells Penney as soon as it knows, and no later than the day before. Penney may adjust the schedule as the job requires and will give Subcontractor as much notice as it can."},{"title":"Daily log, photos and job records","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nEach day Subcontractor works on a Penney job, it posts a daily log in the Penney sub portal: what was done, who was there, and photos of the work.\nFor time-and-material Work the daily log must also show the workers and hours for that day, and it is required for payment: Penney pays time-and-material invoices only for days with a daily log posted in the portal, unless Penney agrees otherwise in writing.\nPhotos and posts Subcontractor adds in the portal become part of Penney's job record. Penney may keep them, use them for the job, and show them to the Owner in the Owner's portal. Subcontractor will not post photos of an Owner's home publicly without written permission from Penney and the Owner."},{"title":"Site rules","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nOn every Penney job, Subcontractor:\n- works during [FILL IN: normal site hours, e.g. 7:00 AM to 5:00 PM, Monday to Friday], unless the Work Order, the association or the town sets other hours, or Penney approves otherwise;\n- in condominiums and multi-unit buildings, follows the association's rules, protects common areas, and gives Penney at least [FILL IN: how much notice Penney gets, e.g. 24 hours'] notice before loud work or any water, power or gas shut-off;\n- protects floors, finishes, fixtures, landscaping and the Owner's belongings from its work, and controls dust;\n- cleans up its debris every day and leaves its area broom-clean, using the dumpster Penney provides or hauling its debris away;\n- follows OSHA and all safety rules, provides fall protection and safety equipment for its workers, and reports any injury or damage to Penney the same day;\n- does not smoke, vape, use alcohol or drugs, play loud music, or bring pets on site, and parks where Penney or the Owner says;\n- keeps door codes, lockbox codes and keys private, and returns keys when the Work is done.\nSubcontractor repairs, or pays for, any damage it or its workers cause to the property or to other trades' work."},{"title":"Dealing with the Owner","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nPenney is Subcontractor's customer on every Penney job, and Subcontractor takes direction only from Penney. If the Owner asks for extra work, a change or a price, Subcontractor politely refers the Owner to Penney and tells Penney.\nSubcontractor does not discuss its prices, Penney's prices, or the terms of this Agreement with the Owner, and does not offer to do work for the Owner directly. During the Work and for [FILL IN: non-solicitation period, e.g. 12 months] after it ends, Subcontractor will not contract directly with an Owner it met through Penney for work at that property unless Penney agrees in writing.\nNothing here stops Subcontractor from being courteous or answering an Owner's safety question."},{"title":"Confidentiality","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor keeps confidential Penney's prices, markup, estimates, bids and customer list; the terms of this Agreement and its Work Orders; and Owners' personal information, addresses, schedules and access codes. Subcontractor uses them only to do the Work and shares them only with its own workers who need them. This continues after this Agreement ends."},{"title":"Changes","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nNo change in scope, price or schedule is binding unless it is written in a Sub Change Order signed by Penney before the changed work starts. Work Subcontractor does without a signed Sub Change Order is at its own cost, even if the Owner asked for it.\nThe only exception is an emergency that threatens people or property: Subcontractor may act to make it safe, must tell Penney the same day, and the parties then write up a Sub Change Order. A Sub Change Order may add to the price, or reduce it as a credit to Penney."},{"title":"Invoices and payment","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor invoices through the Penney sub portal (or another way Penney gives in writing), showing the Work Order number, the work completed, and the amount due under the Work Order's payment schedule.\nPenney reviews each invoice and pays approved amounts [FILL IN: payment timing, e.g. within 30 days after Penney approves the invoice]. Penney makes no payment while it lacks Subcontractor's signed W-9 or a current certificate of insurance.\nAfter telling Subcontractor why in writing, Penney may hold back from a payment: amounts for work that is defective or not done; the cost of cleanup or damage Subcontractor caused and did not fix after notice; and amounts needed to cover a lien or claim by Subcontractor's own workers or suppliers. Penney pays the held amount once the reason is resolved. A payment is not acceptance of defective work."},{"title":"Retainage","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nPenney holds back [FILL IN: retainage percent of each payment, e.g. 0% or 10%] of each payment as retainage. Penney pays the retainage with the final payment, after Subcontractor's Work is complete, has passed any required inspection, and Subcontractor has given Penney the closeout items the Work Order lists (for example warranties, manuals and permit sign-offs)."},{"title":"Lien waivers","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nWhen Penney pays, Subcontractor will, if asked, sign a lien waiver on the statutory form in M.G.L. c.254 §32, covering only the amount Penney has actually paid. Penney will never ask Subcontractor to waive its lien rights before it is paid; a waiver given in advance of payment is void under c.254 §32.\nSubcontractor pays its own workers and suppliers for the Work on time and, if Penney asks, gives Penney their lien waivers for amounts Penney has paid. If one of them records or threatens a lien for work Penney has already paid Subcontractor for, Subcontractor has it released at its own cost within [FILL IN: days for Subcontractor to clear a lower-tier lien after Penney's notice, e.g. 10 days]."},{"title":"Warranty","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor warrants that its Work is free from defects in workmanship for [FILL IN: warranty period, standard is one (1) year] after the Owner's project is substantially complete, or longer if a Work Order requires.\nSubcontractor fixes defects in its Work at its own cost, starting within [FILL IN: days to start a warranty fix after Penney's notice, e.g. 7 days] after Penney tells it, or within [FILL IN: response time for urgent problems, e.g. 24 hours] for a leak, a safety issue or anything causing damage. Manufacturers' warranties on materials Subcontractor supplies pass through to the Owner, and Subcontractor gives Penney the paperwork. This warranty does not cover damage by others, misuse or normal wear."},{"title":"Indemnity","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nTo the extent caused by the negligence, recklessness or intentional wrongful act or omission of Subcontractor, its workers, agents, suppliers or lower-tier subcontractors, Subcontractor will indemnify and hold harmless Penney and the Owner from claims, damages, losses and reasonable attorney's fees for bodily injury, death or damage to property arising out of the Work.\nAs M.G.L. c.149 §29C requires, Subcontractor never has to indemnify Penney, the Owner or anyone else for injury or damage not caused by Subcontractor or those working for it, including anything caused by Penney's or the Owner's own negligence. Where several parties share fault, Subcontractor's share is limited to its own part. This section continues after this Agreement ends."},{"title":"Lower-tier subcontractors and assignment","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor will not subcontract any of the Work, or assign this Agreement or a Work Order, without Penney's written OK. A lower-tier subcontractor Penney approves is bound by the same terms, insurance and workers' compensation requirements, and Subcontractor stays responsible for its work and for paying it."},{"title":"Owner and association requirements","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nA Work Order may list requirements from Penney's contract with the Owner or from a condominium association, for example insurance certificates naming the Owner or association, protection of common areas, or work hours. For its part of the work, Subcontractor follows those requirements and owes Penney the same duties Penney owes the Owner or association under them, but only for its own Work and for damage or claims caused by Subcontractor, its workers or its lower-tier subcontractors, and always subject to the Indemnity section. Where such a requirement says \"the Contractor\" or \"its subcontractors\", for Subcontractor that means Subcontractor and its own lower-tier subcontractors."},{"title":"Ending a work order or this agreement","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nPenney may end a Work Order for cause if Subcontractor does not perform the Work as agreed, misses confirmed dates without good reason, works without required insurance or licenses, or breaks the site rules, and does not fix the problem within [FILL IN: cure period after written notice, e.g. 2 business days] after Penney's written notice (or right away for a safety violation). Penney may then finish the Work with others and deduct the reasonable extra cost from what it owes Subcontractor.\nPenney may also end a Work Order for its convenience, or because the Owner ends or suspends Penney's contract, by written notice. Penney then pays Subcontractor for Work properly done up to the notice and for materials ordered for the job that cannot be returned, but not profit on work not performed.\nSubcontractor may end a Work Order if Penney does not pay an approved, undisputed amount within [FILL IN: days Penney has to pay after the sub's written overdue notice before the sub may end the Work Order, e.g. 10 days] after Subcontractor's written notice that it is overdue.\nEither party may end this Agreement for future Work Orders by written notice. Work Orders already signed continue until finished."},{"title":"Disputes","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nIf a disagreement comes up, Ryan Penney and Subcontractor's owner will first try in good faith to settle it by talking it through, for at least [FILL IN: good-faith negotiation period before court or arbitration, e.g. 14 days] after either one raises it in writing. During that time Subcontractor keeps working on undisputed Work and Penney keeps paying undisputed amounts.\nIf that does not settle it, the dispute will be decided by [FILL IN: choose one: \"the state courts sitting in Essex County, Massachusetts\" or \"binding arbitration under the Construction Industry Arbitration Rules of the American Arbitration Association, held in Essex County, Massachusetts\"].\nNothing in this section limits either party's rights under the Massachusetts mechanic's lien law (M.G.L. c.254)."},{"title":"Governing law and the rest","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nMassachusetts law governs this Agreement and every Work Order and Sub Change Order under it. Together they are the entire agreement between the parties about the Work and replace earlier discussions; a quote or proposal is part of it only to the extent a Work Order quotes it. Changes to this Agreement must be in writing and signed by both parties.\nIf a court finds part of this Agreement unenforceable, the rest still applies and that part is enforced as far as the law allows. Notices may be given by email to the address each party gives the other, or through the Penney sub portal."},{"title":"Electronic signatures and records","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nThe parties agree to sign this Agreement, Work Orders and Sub Change Orders electronically in the Penney sub portal, under the Massachusetts Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (M.G.L. c.110G) and the federal ESIGN Act. An electronic signature here has the same effect as a handwritten one. Penney keeps the signed record, and Subcontractor can download it from the portal at any time."}]$penney_w9$::jsonb,
       acceptance_text = $penney_w9$[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]
By signing, Subcontractor agrees to this Master Subcontract Agreement with Penney Construction, Inc. It applies to every Work Order Penney issues to Subcontractor during the calendar year shown on page one, and keeps applying to those Work Orders until their work is finished, warranted and paid.$penney_w9$,
       esign_consent_text = $penney_w9$[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]
I am authorized to sign for the company named on this document.
I agree to do business with Penney Construction, Inc. electronically. Typing my name and tapping Sign is my signature on this document, with the same effect as signing a paper copy (Massachusetts Uniform Electronic Transactions Act, M.G.L. c.110G, and the federal ESIGN Act).
I can open and download this document in the sub portal at any time, and Penney will give me a paper copy free if I ask. I can stop signing electronically for future documents by telling Penney in writing; that does not change documents already signed.
My typed name, the date and time, my portal login, and my device and internet address are recorded with my signature and printed on the signed copy.$penney_w9$
 where kind = 'master'
   and version = 1
   and status = 'draft'
   and sections = '[]'::jsonb
   and acceptance_text = '[FILL IN: acceptance statement]'
   and esign_consent_text = '[FILL IN: e-sign consent]';

update public.sub_agreement_templates
   set title = $penney_w9$Work order$penney_w9$,
       sections = $penney_w9$[{"title":"Under the master agreement","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nThis Work Order is issued under the Master Subcontract Agreement named above, and every term of that Agreement applies to it. The scope, price, dates and Owner requirements on this Work Order are specific to this job and control over the Agreement if the two conflict, except that they never enlarge Subcontractor's indemnity beyond the Agreement's Indemnity section or its lien waivers beyond the Lien waivers section."},{"title":"Scope","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor performs the scope shown above, as written. Anything listed under exclusions, or not written in the scope, is not part of this Work Order. If Subcontractor thinks something its scope needs is missing or unclear, it asks Penney in writing before starting that part.\nUnless the scope says Penney or the Owner supplies something, Subcontractor supplies the labor, tools, equipment and materials its scope needs."},{"title":"Price","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nThe price basis is shown above.\n- Lump sum: the price covers the entire scope.\n- Unit price: Subcontractor is paid the unit price for the quantity actually installed, as measured with Penney.\n- Time and material: Subcontractor is paid the rate shown for hours actually worked on site, as recorded in its daily portal log, plus materials Penney approved in advance at Subcontractor's actual cost [FILL IN: markup allowed on time-and-material materials, e.g. \"with no markup\" or \"plus 10%\"]. The total never goes over the not-to-exceed amount without a signed Sub Change Order, and Subcontractor tells Penney before it reaches [FILL IN: not-to-exceed warning point, e.g. 80%] of that amount."},{"title":"Payment schedule","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nPenney pays as the payment schedule above shows, under the invoice, retainage and lien waiver terms of the Master Subcontract Agreement. Time-and-material days are paid only when that day's daily log is posted in the portal."},{"title":"Dates","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nSubcontractor works within the dates shown, as confirmed in the sub portal. Dates move only when Penney reschedules them."},{"title":"Changes","body":"[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]\nAny change to this scope, price or dates needs a Sub Change Order signed by Penney before the changed work starts."}]$penney_w9$::jsonb,
       acceptance_text = $penney_w9$[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]
By signing, Subcontractor accepts this Work Order, with the scope, price and dates shown, under its Master Subcontract Agreement with Penney Construction, Inc.$penney_w9$,
       esign_consent_text = $penney_w9$[FILL IN: DRAFT for Ryan Penney's review. Delete this line after you read this section.]
I am authorized to sign for the company named on this document.
